<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kenya Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1963 CE · capital Nairobi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British settler colony in Kenya, shaped by land alienation in the White Highlands and shaken by the Mau Mau uprising.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1920 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> annexed the interior of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-africa-protectorate">East Africa Protectorate</a> as Kenya Colony, while the coastal strip, still nominally under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar</a>, became the Kenya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>. Annexation gave the white settler community, concentrated in the central highlands, more political weight. Nairobi remained the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Europeans held the best farmland and dominated the legislative council, while Africans faced taxes, pass laws and restrictions on growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>. Indians campaigned for equal rights. African political protest began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-thuku">Harry Thuku</a> in 1921 and grew through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kikuyu-people">Kikuyu</a> associations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-mau-rebellion">Mau Mau uprising</a> from 1952 led to a state of emergency under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evelyn-baring-1st-baron-howick-of-glendale">Evelyn Baring</a> and mass detention.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The emergency ended in 1960, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> conferences opened the way to majority rule. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kenya">Kenya</a> became independent on 12 December 1963 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a>, who had been imprisoned during the emergency.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kenya-colony.jpg" alt="Kenya Colony" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dag · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kenya_(1921%E2%80%931963).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1963 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nairobi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Evelyn Baring</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony and Protectorate of Kenya, Kenya Protectorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=kenya-colony" aria-label="Kenya Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5703 2226 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kenya Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6023 2482L6123 2523M6023 2482L6101 2585M6023 2482L4997 651M6023 2482L6023 2482"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6123 2523h0M6101 2585h0M4997 651h0M6023 2482h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6023 2482h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6023 2482h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nairobi. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=kenya-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
